    id: "d360149389ef4dd0a4d1",
    cat: "geopolitica",
    categories: ["geopolitica"],
    title: "Trump says FBI investigating Nvidia boss's affair with alleged Chinese spy",
    summary: "The US president said he spoke with Jensen Huang on Monday and asked him about the affair with the alleged spy.",
    preview: "US President Donald Trump has said the FBI is investigating the affair between the chief executive of Nvidia and an alleged Chinese spy. Trump said he spoke to Jensen Huang on Monday following reports that the US believed he had been targeted in a Chinese espionage campaign. \"I asked him that question, 'Did you have any idea she was a spy?' He said 'I had no idea she was a spy' and I really believe him,\" Trump told reporters.",
    body: "US President Donald Trump has said the FBI is investigating the affair between the chief executive of Nvidia and an alleged Chinese spy.\n\nTrump said he spoke to Jensen Huang on Monday following reports that the US believed he had been targeted in a Chinese espionage campaign.\n\n\"I asked him that question, 'Did you have any idea she was a spy?' He said 'I had no idea she was a spy' and I really believe him,\" Trump told reporters.\n\nBeijing has rejected claims that Huang was targeted, with its foreign ministry saying the allegations were \"entirely groundless, speculative and intended to smear and malign China\".\n\nNvidia, which has commissioned an independent investigation into the affair involving Alice Huang, alleged to be a Chinese spy, said it had no reason to believe the company's secrets or national security were compromised.\n\nThe company is the world's second most valuable company after Apple and designs computer chips that have become central to AI technology. The revelations knocked about $250bn (£190bn) off Nvidia's value on Monday as its share price fell by more than 4%.\n\nTrump later told reporters at the White House that the FBI was now looking into the issue, but did not elaborate on the probe or when it was launched. The FBI declined to comment when contacted by the BBC.\n\nUS intelligence officials believe Alice Huang, also known as Huang Le, was a spy for Beijing, according to a New York Times report.\n\nThe report said Secretary of State Marco Rubio sent a memo to top officials in Washington last spring warning that the Nvidia boss was in a close relationship with a Chinese spy.\n\n- What we know so far about Jensen Huang's affair with suspected Chinese spy\n\n- Published2 hours ago\n\nAlice Huang was managing director of Singaporean company Megaspeed, which has had dealings with Nvidia, the company at the centre at the development of AI worldwide through its microchips. She is not related to the Nvidia chief.\n\nTrump's remarks are the first indication of whether Nvidia's Huang knew about the woman's alleged ties to Chinese espionage.\n\nThe American firm, co-founded by Huang, is also a major focus in the AI rivalry between the US and China, with officials in Washington treating the company's exports to Beijing as a matter of national security.\n\nHuang and Nvidia were also sued on Monday in a proposed class action lawsuit in California accusing them of defrauding shareholders by concealing the chief executive's ties with a suspected Chinese spy.\n\nThe lawsuit alleges that the firm lied to the media about Huang's relationship with Alice Huang, \"who is suspected of seeking to help smuggle Nvidia products to China.\"\n\nThe BBC has contacted Nvidia for comment on the lawsuit.\n\n'He said he had no idea she was a spy'\n\nAt the White House, Trump was asked multiple times, including by the BBC, when he was made aware of the relationship. He said he was informed \"over a period of time\" but was not specific.\n\nTrump said he thought Huang was an \"honorable guy and I feel very confident that he will be in good shape\" after the investigations.\n\n\"I hope there's no problem, I believe he is suffering greatly,\" the president said.\n\nHe said he spoke to Huang on the phone on Monday \"just to see if he's feeling alright, how you doing, and I asked him about that and he said he absolutely had no idea that the person in question was a spy\".\n\nNvidia designs advanced computer chips that power AI models and is at the centre of the US and China's technology race - a key reason why Huang's affair has sparked national security fears.\n\nTrump has raised concerns that allowing China to lead in AI would supercharge Beijing's military and surveillance capabilities.\n\nThe president added he was \"sure\" Chinese leader Xi Jinping would \"ask me about it\" when they next met.\n\nHuang has admitted he was \"unfaithful\" to his wife and apologised for hurting her and their children.\n\nHe said on Monday that he was \"committed to protecting Nvidia and supporting America's national security\" and would cooperate with the investigation the company had launched.\n\nHuang has also been requested to appear for a joint closed-door briefing for several Congressional committees next week.\n\nIn the letter, the chairmen of the committees said they were seeking a briefing \"on Nvidia's security protocols against foreign adversary espionage and your interactions with a publicly reported People's Republic of China (PRC) intelligence asset: Megaspeed executive Alice Huang\".\n\nIt is unclear whether Huang will appear. Nvidia has declined to comment, while the the BBC has also contacted the committees.\n\n- Zoe Kleinman: Nvidia CEO's affair will cause ripples across US and beyond\n\n- Published4 hours ago\n\n- AI stocks climb after trillions wiped from market values\n\n- Published3 days ago\n\nSome workers the BBC spoke to at Nvidia's Santa Clara headquarters on Monday told the BBC that it was business as usual, despite the intense attention on the company.\n\nNvidia has been around for decades, with Huang founding it with two other engineers in 1993, but its rise to one of the world's most valuable and powerful companies has only been recent.\n\nIt originally designed computer chips for gaming, but its decision to shift focus to AI development turned it into the powerhouse it is today.\n\nIts chips have become so valuable to the AI buildout that the White House previously imposed controls on where its advanced products could be sold.\n\nThe H200, Nvidia's second most advanced semiconductor, had previously been restricted over concerns that it would give China's technology industry an edge over the US.\n\nBut Huang has long been against restricting sales to China, arguing that it would be better for the global rollout of AI to be powered by US technology.\n\nIn January, the Trump administration said it would allow the company to sell its H200 chips to Chinese customers under certain conditions.\n\nNvidia's most advanced AI semiconductors including the GB200 Blackwell super chip remain blocked from the Chinese market.\n\n- Published16 September\n\n- Published14 September",
